{{- /* claude-pattern-c-devcontainer/devcontainer.json — companion container definition for
     Pattern C (home/.chezmoitemplates/claude-pattern-c). Pattern C's `bypassPermissions`
     posture is only safe inside an OS/container boundary — this template defines that
     boundary. It does NOT launch it: copy the rendered devcontainer.json + Dockerfile into
     the target repo's .devcontainer/ and start it with your own devcontainer tooling (VS
     Code, the `devcontainer` CLI, etc.). Launching/orchestrating the container at runtime is
     explicitly out of scope for this change (see design.md's Non-Goals) — a likely follow-up
     extends the `project-session-manager` skill instead.

     `remoteUser` is a non-root account; `workspaceMount`/`mounts` bind ONLY the target
     repository's working tree — never $HOME, ~/.ssh, ~/.aws, or any other credential-bearing
     host path. Do not add host mounts here without re-checking that constraint.

     `.claude/` gets its own named Docker volume, layered on top of the working-tree bind mount
     (`mounts` entries apply after `workspaceMount`, so this shadows the bind-mounted `.claude/`
     subtree). This closes a host-leak: without it, `.claude/settings.local.json` written inside
     the container by `apply-claude-pattern c` is the same inode as the one on the host checkout,
     so Pattern C's `bypassPermissions` posture would silently persist for host (non-container)
     Claude Code sessions after the container exits. With the volume, anything written under
     `.claude/` inside the container lives only in that volume and never reaches the host
     checkout — see design.md Decision 3. `apply-claude-pattern c` independently verifies this
     isolation at write time (checks `/proc/self/mountinfo`) before writing, and refuses if a
     hand-rolled container config bind-mounts `.claude/` instead of using a distinct volume.

     Not part of the persona settings ledger (claude-settings-ledger/claude-settings-modifier)
     and not consumed by `apply-claude-pattern` — that tool only stamps the `permissions`
     JSON block; this container definition is a separate, manually-copied opt-in.

     Test: render and inspect for the absence of any host-credential path, and confirm `mounts`
     includes a `.claude`-scoped volume entry distinct from `workspaceMount`, e.g.
     `chezmoi execute-template < home/.chezmoitemplates/claude-pattern-c-devcontainer/devcontainer.json | jq .`
     Spec: openspec/specs/claude-repo-permission-patterns/
*/ -}}
{
  "name": "claude-pattern-c-sandbox",
  "build": {
    "dockerfile": "Dockerfile"
  },
  "remoteUser": "claude",
  "workspaceFolder": "/workspace",
  "workspaceMount": "source=${localWorkspaceFolder},target=/workspace,type=bind",
  "mounts": [
    "source=claude-pattern-c-dotclaude,target=${containerWorkspaceFolder}/.claude,type=volume"
  ],
  "remoteEnv": {},
  "postCreateCommand": "true"
}
